   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.3. Increased institutional capacity of labour administrations</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">In 2020, following the information and inspection visits of State Labour Service, under the scope of the &quot;Go to Light&quot; campaign, around 220,000 undeclared workers were regularized. 

In 15 June 2021, the State Labour Service became a member of the International Association of Labour Inspection (IALI). 

The draft Law of Ukraine &quot;On Amendments to Certain Legislative Acts Concerning the Procedure for State Supervision (Control) of Observance of Labour Legislation&quot; was sent to the Cabinet of Ministers for consideration (by letter of the Ministry of Economy &#xb9; 4706-02 / 34468-01, dated 01.07.2021).
 With the support of the European Union, the ILO organized online training to over 500 policy makers, legal experts, social partners on International labour standards and best practices on labour inspection and were provided with in-depth knowledge and comprehensive supporting tools. 

In collaboration with the International Training Centre of the ILO, officials of the State Labour Inspectorate and the Ministry of Economy were provided with comprehensive training on &quot;Labour inspection&quot; and &quot;Employment Injury Schemes and Prevention of Occupational Accidents and Diseases&quot;.

National constituents and other stakeholders were provided with 2 sets of technical recommendations on the alignment of the two following draft laws with ILO C81 and C129, on labour inspection.

The ILO conducted an awareness-raising campaign on undeclared work &quot;Go to Light&quot;, that reached over 4,5 million people.</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">1.3.1. Number of member States with institutional frameworks for labour administration that address current and new challenges in the world of work</narrative>
    </title>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 7.4. Increased capacity of constituents to provide adequate labour protection to workers in diverse forms of work arrangements, including on digital labour platforms, and in informal employment</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The National Action Plan to Reduce Undeclared Work (NAP) was adopted on 27 April 2021 by the National Tripartite Working Group (NTWG) led by the State Labour Service. The NTWG integrates representatives, at national and regional levels, of public authorities (Ministry of Economy, State Labour Service, Pension Fund, Ministry of Social Policy, National Police, State Fiscal Service) and social partners (Federation of Employers of Ukraine, Confederation of Employers of Ukraine, and Federation of Trade Unions of Ukraine). Over 500 specialists were involved in its formulation, based on a bottom up approach, in order to collect the proposals from the regions taking into account local and regional peculiarities (sectors with high prevalence of UDW, types of informal employment and UDW, etc.). The proposals from the regions were then consolidated into a single document at the national level. On 15 June 2021, the Committee of Labour Relations and Labour Market of the National Tripartite Social and Economic Council (NTSEC) considered this NAP as the basis for further consideration and adoption by the NTSEC. The NAP-2021 focuses on reducing undeclared work in the informal sector, awareness-raising activities and promotion of a culture of declaration of work among youth, within the framework of the national awareness-raising campaign &quot;Go to Light&quot;.
This campaign was launched in February 2020. Around 4.5 million persons were reached through several means such as: dedicated section on UDW on the State Labour website; a digital campaign, video advertising in inter-city trains, placement of posters, videos and banners in state institutions and social partners&apos; buildings, and video and audio ads by social quota in television and radio. Additionally, there was the Youth stands for declared work initiative, targeting students of above 115 universities all over Ukraine, who learned about the risks of undeclared work through 3 Ukrainian Student Advertising Festivals. The ILO, through the EU funded Project Towards safe, healthy and declared work in Ukraine, contributed to the elaboration and adoption of the NAP (consultations with the experts, workshops, development of the report on UDW and recommendations in 2018, support to the drafting of the national action plan to fight undeclared work in 2018. 42% of the measures proposed have been included in the Plan adopted in 2021 and 28% were/are being implemented in 2019-2021 out of the scope of the adopted Plan).

The ILO, through the EU funded Project Towards safe, healthy and declared work in Ukraine, provided technical support for the design and implementation of the Go to light campaign. The ILO worked in coordination with the NGO Change Communication for the design and implementation phases. The campaign strategy was developed in close collaboration with the State Labour Service and other stakeholders in a participatory manner taking into account the specificity of the Ukrainian content. Implementation of the campaign is being lead by the State Labour Service which is collaborating with other authorities and social partners.</narrative>
   </description>
